This page lists public [[opinion poll]]s conducted for the [[2017 French presidential election]], which was held on 23 April 2017 with a run-off on 7 May 2017.

Unless otherwise noted, all polls listed below are compliant with the regulations of the national polling commission (''Commission nationale des sondages'') and utilize the [[quota sampling|quota method]].

== First round ==
Ifop-Fiducial and OpinionWay polls listed in the tables below starting in February 2017 are "rolling" polls unless otherwise denoted by an asterisk (*).<ref name="Ifop-rolling">{{cite web|title=Rolling 2017 - " La présidentielle en temps réel "|url=http://www.ifop.fr/media/pressdocument/955-1-document_file.pdf|publisher=Ifop|date=31 January 2018|access-date=27 February 2018|archive-date=1 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180301224852/http://www.ifop.fr/media/pressdocument/955-1-document_file.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="OpinionWay-rolling">{{cite web|title=Election présidentielle 2017 : OpinionWay, ORPI, Les Echos et Radio Classique s'associent pour couvrir en continu la campagne présidentielle dès le 6 février|url=https://www.opinion-way.com/fr/sondage-d-opinion/sondages-publies.html?task=document.viewdoc&id=1523|publisher=OpinionWay|date=3 February 2018|access-date=27 February 2018}}</ref> The poll by Belgian pollster Dedicated Research commissioned by ''[[La Libre Belgique]]'' and [[RTBF]] and published on 20 February 2017, marked with two asterisks (**) in the table below, was not subject to French regulations.

Polls marked with three asterisks (***) from Scan Research/Le Terrain use [[computer-assisted telephone interviewing|CATI]] and [[random digit dialing|random number dialing]], unlike all other pollsters, which conduct online surveys using the [[quota sampling|quota method]]. The polling commission published notices for each of the two polls conducted by Scan Research/Le Terrain.<ref name="SRLTMarch">{{cite web|title=Notice technique : Intentions de vote du 23 au 27 mars 2017 (Sondage par téléphone)|url=http://www.commission-des-sondages.fr/notices/files/notices/2017/avril/8383-pres-le-terrain.pdf|publisher=Commission des sondages|date=27 March 2017|access-date=27 February 2018}}</ref><ref name="SRLTApril">{{cite web|title=Notice technique : Intentions de vote du 13 au 15 avril 2017 (Sondage par téléphone)|url=http://www.commission-des-sondages.fr/notices/files/notices/2017/avril/8429-pres-le-terrain.pdf|publisher=Commission des sondages|date=15 April 2017|access-date=27 February 2018}}</ref>

[[Alain Juppé]], who lost [[The Republicans (France) presidential primary, 2016|the primary of the right and centre]] to Fillon, was floated to replace him as a result of the [[Fillon affair]] (Penelopegate). Though tested in some hypothetical polls, Juppé announced on 6 March that he would not be a candidate, regardless of what happened with Fillon.<ref>{{cite news|author1=Alexandre Lemarié|author2=Matthieu Goar|title=Juppé renonce, la droite au bord de la rupture|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/03/06/juppe-renonce-la-droite-au-bord-de-la-rupture_5089836_4854003.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=6 March 2017|access-date=21 March 2017}}</ref>

=== Graphical summary ===
The averages in the graphs below were constructed using polls listed below conducted by the eight major French pollsters. The graphs are smoothed 14-day weighted moving averages, using only the most recent poll conducted by any given pollster within that range (each poll weighted based on recency).

[[François Bayrou]] of the [[Democratic Movement (France)|Democratic Movement]] (MoDem) renounced a potential candidacy on 22 February 2017 and instead proposed an alliance with [[Emmanuel Macron]], which he accepted.<ref>{{cite news|title=Présidentielle : Bayrou et Macron, une alliance sous conditions|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/02/22/presidentielle-fin-du-suspense-autour-de-la-candidature-de-francois-bayrou_5083592_4854003.html|agency=Agence France-Presse|newspaper=Le Monde|date=22 February 2017|access-date=6 March 2017}}</ref> [[Yannick Jadot]] of [[Europe Ecology – The Greens]] (EELV) announced that he would withdraw his candidacy and endorsed [[Benoît Hamon]] on 23 February after negotiating a common platform with the Socialist nominee;<ref>{{cite news|title=Yannick Jadot se retire de la course à la présidentielle et rallie Benoît Hamon|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/election-presidentielle-2017/article/2017/02/23/yannick-jadot-se-retire-de-la-course-a-la-presidentielle-et-rallie-benoit-hamon_5084560_4854003.html|agency=Agence France-Presse|newspaper=Le Monde|date=23 February 2017|access-date=6 March 2017}}</ref> the agreement was approved by the EELV primary voters on 26 February.<ref>{{cite news|title=Présidentielle : les électeurs écologistes approuvent l'accord entre Hamon et Jadot|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/politique/article/2017/02/26/presidentielle-les-electeurs-ecologistes-approuvent-a-pres-de-80-l-accord-entre-hamon-et-jadot_5085810_823448.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=26 February 2017|access-date=6 March 2017}}</ref>

